A distributed web infrastructure consists of multiple servers to improve availability and scalability.

The infrastructure includes:
- 1 load balancer (HAProxy)
- 2 servers, each containing:
  - a web server (Nginx)
  - an application server
  - application code
  - a database (MySQL)

Why adding a load balancer:
The load balancer distributes incoming traffic across multiple servers to improve performance and ensure high availability.

Load balancing algorithm:
The load balancer can use a Round Robin algorithm, which distributes requests evenly across all servers in sequence.

Active-Active vs Active-Passive:
In an Active-Active setup, all servers handle traffic simultaneously.
In an Active-Passive setup, one server is active while the other is on standby and only takes over if the active server fails.

Database Primary-Replica (Master-Slave):
The Primary database handles write operations (INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE).
The Replica database replicates data from the Primary and is mainly used for read operations.

Difference between Primary and Replica:
The Primary node processes writes and ensures data consistency.
The Replica node handles read queries and improves performance by reducing load on the Primary.

Single Point of Failure (SPOF):
- The load balancer is a SPOF (if it fails, the system becomes inaccessible)
- The Primary database is a SPOF (if it fails, writes cannot be performed)

Security issues:
- No firewall to protect the servers
- No HTTPS, so data is transmitted in plain text

No monitoring:
There is no monitoring system to detect failures, performance issues, or security incidents.
